<p class=3DMsoNormal><font size=3D2 face=3DArial><span =
style=3D'font-size:10.0pt;
font-family:Arial'>Radiant Data Server (RDS) is a distributed =
replicating file
system that makes it possible to share data with network-wide =
consistency
between any <span class=3DGramE>number</span> of sites. RDS =
&quot;nodes&quot; are
connected to one another using standard TCP/IP networks. Nodes form a
peer-to-peer network known as the consistency group. <span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>All nodes within the same =
consistency group share
the same data and multiple consistency groups can be formed to share =
only
specific data between specific nodes, if desired RDS can be configured =
to use
256-bit AES encryption on a node by node basis. Unlike NFS, <span =
class=3DSpellE>InterMezzo</span>,
Coda and AFS, each server that is part of a consistency group is immune =
to
network failure and disconnected operation, since all of the data is =
stored on
a local disk allowing for read/write operations on all your data to =
occur even
when the network fails.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; =
</span>When the network
comes back up the RDS system runs a consistency protocol that ensures =
that all
modified data is then replicated to all members of the consistency =
group. <span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>When data is modified, only the =
actual changes
within the data are replicated between the nodes, ensuring maximum =
performance
with minimal network overhead<o:p></o:p></span></font></p>

